{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat AGENTS.md && git status --short'","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n M packages/axylo/src/scale/band.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/scale/domain.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/scale/linear.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/scale/nice.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/scale/time.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/scale/zone.ts\n M packages/axylo/src/theme/resolve.ts\n M packages/axylo/test/scale/domain.test.ts\n M packages/axylo/test/scale/linear.test.ts\n M packages/axylo/test/scale/time.test.ts\n?? packages/axylo/src/render/canvas.ts\n?? packages/axylo/src/render/index.ts\n?? packages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts\n?? packages/axylo/src/render/text.ts\n?? packages/axylo/test/browser/render/\n?? packages/axylo/test/format/review.test.ts\n?? packages/axylo/test/render/\n?? packages/axylo/test/scale/review.test.ts\n?? packages/axylo/test/theme/\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.events.jsonl\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.prompt.md\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.report.md\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/view-scene.events.jsonl\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/view-scene.prompt.md\n?? reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/view-scene.report.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'F1\\\\b|F2\\\\b|F5\\\\b|F7\\\\b|F8\\\\b|F9\\\\b|F10\\\\b|L2\\\\b|L5\\\\b|A5\\\\b|C2\\\\b|C4\\\\b|3\\\\.5|3\\\\.6|parts|[Vv]iew|[Ss]cene' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'F1\\\\b|F2\\\\b|F5\\\\b|F7\\\\b|F8\\\\b|F9\\\\b|F10\\\\b|L2\\\\b|L5\\\\b|A5\\\\b|C2\\\\b|C4\\\\b|3\\\\.5|3\\\\.6|parts|[Vv]iew|[Ss]cene' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/02-product-spec.md:3:Revision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)), then updated with the owner's decisions of the same day: scatter is in 1.0, a compact variant is in 1.0, and the mark renderer is decided by measurement in M1.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:54:| Area | Single, stacked | How did the total and its parts change? | 1.0 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:61:The Phase 1 harness exercised line, bar and donut in all ten libraries (donut in nine; uPlot has none). Area and scatter are added on judgment; Phase 1 found no usage data by chart type. Two reviewers argued for fewer types and one for a compact variant; the owner decided on all five types plus the compact variant. Scatter alone needs two-dimensional hit testing and its own navigation model, so it is built last and carries the most schedule risk.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:63:Each type has its own rules for picking, navigation and limits. They are in the type matrix in section 3.6, and a type does not ship until its row is fully tested.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:74:| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:94:| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:95:| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:96:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:97:| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:98:| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A gap is drawn whenever at least one whole column contains no valid sample. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:100:| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, reference lines and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:101:| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices, the seven largest are drawn and the rest are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; the table lists every category. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:102:| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker, and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:103:| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:112:| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:115:| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:124:| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". When it cannot fit, the same content appears in a fixed readout under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:129:| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:140:| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:141:| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:142:| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:148:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:183:The split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:185:### 3.6 Type matrix\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:193:| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:204:| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:206:| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:213:Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:222:| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:223:| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:255:\"Status\" records what the reviews changed.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:257:| Decision | What decided it | Status after review |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:259:| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:260:| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:262:| The normalized data, not the drawing, is the source for values, table, keyboard and summary | Reviews: a simplified drawing cannot return the values it dropped. | New. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:267:| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:295:| Is the palette that meets A5 one people want to look at? | Hand-tune from the search output with the thresholds in CI. | M5. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:3:Revision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)) and the owner's decisions of the same day (scatter and a compact variant are in 1.0; the mark renderer is decided in M1).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:5:This plan builds the product defined in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md). Criterion IDs (F1, L1, A2, DX4, P1 …) refer to that spec; finding IDs (R5, D2 …) refer to [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:85:| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:86:| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:88:| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:103:| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:112:chart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:127:| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:132:The registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:172:options ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:179:The **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:184:| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:187:| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:197:| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:198:| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:199:| scene | Layout or view model. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:200:| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:208:**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:210:M1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:212:**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:226:    legend toggles · zoom in · zoom out · reset · pause · view as table\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:251:Tooling: TypeScript strict; ESM output with `.d.ts`, plus IIFE files for `<script>` use; Vitest; fast-check for property tests; Playwright on Chromium, Firefox and WebKit; axe-core; size-limit on the packed distribution; API Extractor so that public API changes fail CI until reviewed; Changesets.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:272:- **Renderer comparison:** the mark renderer built twice (Canvas 2D and SVG paths) behind the same scene, measured as in 2.3.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:277:- `trend` renders in three engines at 720 and 340 px; scene snapshot; resize in both directions with no call from the page.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:286:- The diagnostic registry (F3, F12); missing values and absent steps (F2); the three temporal types (F4); formatting (F6); domains (F7); sorting and duplicates (F11); `validate()`.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:291:- Property tests: no scene path contains a non-finite coordinate; no segment crosses a missing index; domains obey F7.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:311:- The pattern chosen in M1, built fully: names and summary (A1), keyboard (A2), point text (A3), table with paging (A4), non-color cues (A5), reflow (A6), streaming behavior (A7), messages and language (A10); single-pointer zoom controls (I3, I4); reduced motion on update (I5).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:324:Each type is done when its column of the type matrix (spec 3.6) and every applicable row of the acceptance matrix pass, including the accessibility and performance rows. Types are finished one at a time, in this order: bar, area, donut, the compact variant, scatter.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:329:- `parts` fixture: legend carries values and percentages; ninth slice, zero slice, negative slice and all-zero cases.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:342:- F5's assertions on kept samples, including the adversarial same-column cases.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:362:- The Phase 1 comparison regenerated with Axylo beside the ten libraries, with the same harness and the corrections from the review, and published with its limits.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:364:- Independent review of the acceptance evidence before 1.0 is tagged, by a reviewer who did not write the code under review.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:375:| Property | fast-check | Invariants that must hold for any data: F1, F2, F5, F7, F11, L1. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:376:| Scene snapshot | Vitest, injected text metrics | Geometry and text for each fixture and width, without a browser. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:379:| Visual | Screenshots per operating system | What the scene cannot see: antialiasing, state leaks. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:404:| Second y axis | Often misleading; needs a design that keeps F1. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:408:| Server-side rendering to SVG | The scene makes it possible. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
