{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts'","aggregated_output":"/**\n * Internal contracts of Axylo. Every module is written against these types and against nothing else in\n * another module. Changing this file is an architecture change: it needs a reason recorded in the commit.\n *\n * Pipeline (docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md, section 2):\n *\n *   options ─► normalize ─► Frame ─┬─► View ─► Layout ─► Scene ─► MarkRenderer / TextLayer\n *                │ diagnostics      ├─► lookup (exact values)\n *                                   ├─► summary, table\n *                                   └─► focus model\n *\n * Rule that must never be broken: exact values, the table, keyboard navigation, tooltips, summaries and\n * events read the Frame. The Scene is geometry for drawing and may be simplified. Nothing reads values\n * back from the Scene.\n */\nimport type { Diagnostic, XValue } from '../public';\n\n// ───────────────────────────── diagnostics ─────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * What a diagnostic did to the chart. Drives the reader's note (spec F12): only 'omitted' and 'changed'\n * count towards \"N values not shown\"; a warning about contrast or an unknown option does not.\n */\nexport type DiagnosticEffect = 'none' | 'omitted' | 'changed' | 'series-disabled' | 'chart-error';\n\n/** A diagnostic before the chart adds its own name. */\nexport type DiagnosticInput = Omit<Diagnostic, 'chart'> & {\n  readonly effect: DiagnosticEffect;\n  /**\n   * 'call': found while validating a create, update or append call; a `strict` chart throws from that call.\n   * 'presentation': found later (a contrast check after a CSS change, a formatter that throws on a lazily\n   * built table cell, a label that no longer fits after a resize); reported when found, never thrown.\n   */\n  readonly origin: 'call' | 'presentation';\n};\n\nexport interface DiagnosticSink {\n  /** Adds one diagnostic. The sink merges by code, field and effect: counts are added, example rows capped at 10. */\n  add(d: DiagnosticInput): void;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── frame ─────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * What an x value is.\n *  number   – a plain measure.\n *  time     – an instant. Position = epoch milliseconds (UTC). Shown in `XColumn.timeZone`.\n *  date     – a calendar date, month or year. Position = epoch milliseconds of that date at 00:00 UTC;\n *             always formatted in UTC, so it reads the same in every viewer zone.\n *  wall     – a date-time written without an offset. Position = the written fields read as if UTC;\n *             always formatted in UTC (\"shown as written\").\n *  category – a name. Position = index in first-seen order (bar, line) or given order (donut).\n */\nexport type XKind = 'number' | 'time' | 'date' | 'wall' | 'category';\n\n/** State of one slot of an aligned series. */\nexport const SLOT_VALUE = 0;         // a finite number\nexport const SLOT_NULL = 1;          // explicit null (or NaN in a typed column, or an empty string): \"no data\", silent\nexport const SLOT_INVALID = 2;       // present but not plottable (NaN or infinity in a row, a stray string): reported\nexport const SLOT_NO_ROW = 3;        // this series has no observation at this x and none was expected; neighbours may be joined across it\nexport const SLOT_GAP = 4;           // an expected step of this series is absent (AX105): never joined across\nexport const SLOT_ABSENT_FIELD = 5;  // the row exists but the field's key is absent or undefined (AX102)\nexport type SlotState = 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5;\n/**\n * `missing: 'zero'` on a value field is applied by normalization: slots that would be SLOT_NULL, SLOT_NO_ROW,\n * SLOT_GAP or SLOT_ABSENT_FIELD become SLOT_VALUE with y = 0 and row = -1. SLOT_INVALID is never turned into zero.\n */\n\n/** Half-open range of indexes. */\nexport interface IndexRange { readonly start: number; readonly end: number }\n\nexport interface XColumn {\n  readonly kind: XKind;\n  /** Strictly ascending positions, one per slot. For 'category': 0, 1, 2, … */\n  readonly values: Float64Array;\n  /** For 'category': the name of each position. Otherwise null. */\n  readonly categories: readonly string[] | null;\n  /** For 'time': the resolved IANA zone ('UTC', 'America/Los_Angeles', …; 'local' is resolved before it gets here). Otherwise null. */\n  readonly timeZone: string | null;\n  /** For 'date': the precision the values were written in ('2026-02' is a month); the finest one if they are mixed. Otherwise null. */\n  readonly dateUnit: 'day' | 'month' | 'year' | null;\n  /** For 'category': true when the author declared the categories ordered, so the axis may be thinned (spec L1). */\n  readonly ordered: boolean;\n}\n\n/** The caller's data behind a frame, for events and for the table's provenance. */\nexport interface RowSource {\n  /** The caller's row at a source index (materialized on demand for typed columns), or null if it is no longer retained. */\n  rowAt(sourceIndex: number): unknown;\n}\n\n/** One series of an aligned frame: a y value and a state for every x slot. */\nexport interface Series {\n  /** Stable identity: the series value (long form) or the field name (wide form). Used for colors and toggling. */\n  readonly key: string;\n  /** Name shown to the reader. */\n  readonly label: string;\n  /** The value field this series was read from, for diagnostics. */\n  readonly field: string;\n  readonly unit: string | null;\n  readonly valueScale: 'percent' | 'ratio' | null;\n  /** False when the series cannot be drawn (duplicate x, AX108). It stays in the frame so the table and summary can say so. */\n  readonly drawable: boolean;\n  /** y per slot. NaN wherever `slot` is not SLOT_VALUE. May be a view on the caller's typed column. */\n  readonly y: Float64Array | Float32Array;\n  readonly slot: Uint8Array;\n  /** Index of the caller's row each slot came from, -1 where there is none. null means \"slot i came from row i\" (typed columns). */\n  readonly row: Int32Array | null;\n}\n\n/** One series of a point frame (scatter): its own x and y, sorted by x then y. */\nexport interface PointSeries {\n  readonly key: string;\n  readonly label: string;\n  readonly drawable: boolean;\n  readonly x: Float64Array;\n  readonly y: Float64Array;\n  readonly row: Int32Array | null;\n}\n\n/**\n * The normalized, lossless data. Immutable: an update or append produces a new frame with a higher revision.\n * Buffers may be shared between revisions; a streaming store appends into spare capacity and hands out\n * subarray views, so an append does not copy what is already there.\n */\ninterface FrameBase {\n  readonly revision: number;\n  /** Changes when the data is replaced or the encoding changes; survives append and eviction. A DatumRef is only meaningful within one epoch. */\n  readonly epoch: number;\n  readonly source: RowSource;\n}\n\nexport interface AlignedFrame extends FrameBase {\n  readonly shape: 'aligned';\n  readonly x: XColumn;\n  readonly series: readonly Series[];\n  /** Number of x slots. */\n  readonly length: number;\n  readonly xLabel: string;\n  readonly yLabel: string | null;\n}\n\nexport interface PointFrame extends FrameBase {\n  readonly shape: 'points';\n  readonly xKind: Exclude<XKind, 'category'>;\n  readonly timeZone: string | null;\n  readonly series: readonly PointSeries[];\n  readonly xLabel: string;\n  readonly yLabel: string;\n  readonly xUnit: string | null;\n  readonly yUnit: string | null;\n}\n\n/** Donut data: every category the caller gave, in the given order, before any grouping into \"Other\". */\nexport interface DonutFrame extends FrameBase {\n  readonly shape: 'donut';\n  readonly labels: readonly string[];\n  /** NaN where `slot` is not SLOT_VALUE. */\n  readonly values: Float64Array;\n  readonly slot: Uint8Array;\n  readonly row: Int32Array | null;\n  readonly valueLabel: string;\n  readonly unit: string | null;\n  readonly valueScale: 'percent' | 'ratio' | null;\n}\n\nexport type Frame = AlignedFrame | PointFrame | DonutFrame;\n\n/**\n * Join rule for lines and areas (spec F2). Two consecutive SLOT_VALUE slots i < j of one series are joined\n * if and only if every slot strictly between them is SLOT_NO_ROW. A value with no joined neighbour on either\n * side is an isolated point and must be drawn as a visible point.\n */\n\n// ───────────────────────────── formatting ─────────────────────────────\n\nexport interface Formatters {\n  /** Value as readers see it in tooltip, table and announcements: full precision per spec F6, unit appended. */\n  value(seriesIndex: number, v: number): string;\n  /** x as readers see it in tooltip, table and announcements. For 'time' it includes the zone abbreviation. */\n  x(position: number): string;\n  /** Text for a slot that is not a value: \"No data\", \"No observation\". */\n  missing(state: SlotState): string;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── scales and ticks ─────────────────────────────\n\nexport interface ContinuousScale {\n  readonly type: 'linear' | 'time';\n  readonly domain: readonly [number, number];\n  /** Pixel range in CSS px; range[0] maps domain[0]. */\n  readonly range: readonly [number, number];\n  map(value: number): number;\n  invert(pixel: number): number;\n}\n\nexport interface BandScale {\n  readonly type: 'band';\n  readonly count: number;\n  readonly range: readonly [number, number];\n  /** Distance between the starts of adjacent bands. */\n  readonly step: number;\n  /** Width of one band after padding. */\n  readonly bandwidth: number;\n  /** Start pixel of band i. */\n  map(index: number): number;\n  center(index: number): number;\n  /** Band under a pixel, clamped to [0, count - 1]; -1 when count is 0. */\n  invert(pixel: number): number;\n}\n\nexport type XScale = ContinuousScale | BandScale;\n\nexport interface Tick {\n  /** Position in the scale's domain (category: index). */\n  readonly value: number;\n  readonly label: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * Candidate tick sets for one axis, from densest to sparsest. Layout picks the densest set that fits.\n * Every set is a subset of \"nice\" positions for that axis; for a band scale the sets are every k-th category.\n */\nexport type TickCandidates = readonly (readonly Tick[])[];\n\n// ───────────────────────────── view ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** A reference line (one position) or band (two) after its values were turned into axis positions. */\nexport interface ResolvedReference {\n  readonly axis: 'x' | 'y';\n  readonly from: number;\n  /** Equal to `from` for a line. */\n  readonly to: number;\n  readonly label: string;\n}\n\n/** How one series is told apart without color (spec A5): a marker shape and dash patterns. */\nexport interface SeriesCue {\n  readonly marker: MarkerShape;\n  readonly dash: DashStyle;\n}\n\n/**\n * Stacking (spec F10). Positive and negative values accumulate on their own sides of zero.\n * `positive[k]` is the upper boundary of the k-th stacked series' positive part, `positive[k - 1]` (or zero\n * for k = 0) its lower boundary; likewise `negative`. A component that is not SLOT_VALUE contributes zero\n * height and sets `incomplete` for that slot.\n */\nexport interface StackView {\n  /** Frame series indexes in accumulation order (hidden series are left out). */\n  readonly order: readonly number[];\n  readonly positive: readonly Float64Array[];\n  readonly negative: readonly Float64Array[];\n  /** 1 where at least one visible component is missing; null when every stack is complete. */\n  readonly incomplete: Uint8Array | null;\n}\n\ninterface CartesianViewBase {\n  readonly revision: number;\n  /** Visibility per series (legend toggles). */\n  readonly visible: readonly boolean[];\n  readonly cues: readonly SeriesCue[];\n  /** Full x domain of the data (plus the author's domain), and the zoomed domain actually shown. */\n  readonly xFull: readonly [number, number];\n  readonly xDomain: readonly [number, number];\n  /** Value domain after the rules of spec F1 and F7. */\n  readonly yDomain: readonly [number, number];\n  readonly references: readonly ResolvedReference[];\n  readonly compact: boolean;\n}\n\n/** What is currently shown of an aligned frame. */\nexport interface AlignedView extends CartesianViewBase {\n  readonly kind: 'line' | 'area' | 'bar';\n  readonly frame: AlignedFrame;\n  /** True only for horizontal bars. */\n  readonly horizontal: boolean;\n  /** Slots whose x lies inside xDomain. Pointer and keyboard only ever land on these. */\n  readonly visibleSlots: IndexRange;\n  /**\n   * Per series, the slot range to draw: visibleSlots widened on each side to that series' nearest value it\n   * may be joined to, so a line reaches the plot edge. Never used for picking.\n   */\n  readonly drawSlots: readonly IndexRange[];\n  readonly stack: StackView | null;\n}\n\nexport interface ScatterView extends CartesianViewBase {\n  readonly kind: 'scatter';\n  readonly frame: PointFrame;\n  /** Per series, the range of its points whose x lies inside xDomain. */\n  readonly visiblePoints: readonly IndexRange[];\n}\n\n/** One drawn slice or legend entry of a donut (spec F8). */\nexport interface DonutSlice {\n  readonly key: string;\n  readonly label: string;\n  /** Indexes into the donut frame: one for an ordinary slice, several for \"Other (n)\". */\n  readonly members: readonly number[];\n  /** null = \"No data\". */\n  readonly value: number | null;\n  /** Share of the total, 0–1; null when there is no value. */\n  readonly ratio: number | null;\n  /** Start and end angle in radians, clockwise from 12 o'clock; null when nothing is drawn (zero or no data). */\n  readonly angles: readonly [number, number] | null;\n}\n\nexport interface DonutView {\n  readonly kind: 'donut';\n  readonly revision: number;\n  readonly frame: DonutFrame;\n  readonly slices: readonly DonutSlice[];\n  readonly total: number;\n}\n\nexport type View = AlignedView | ScatterView | DonutView;\n\n// ───────────────────────────── text measurement ─────────────────────────────\n\nexport interface TextStyle {\n  /** CSS font shorthand resolved from the page, e.g. \"400 12px Inter, sans-serif\". */\n  readonly font: string;\n  readonly letterSpacing: number;\n  readonly wordSpacing: number;\n  /** Line height in CSS px. */\n  readonly lineHeight: number;\n}\n\n/** Injected into layout. Browser: canvas measureText with the page's computed spacing applied. Tests: fixed metrics. */\nexport interface TextMeasurer {\n  width(text: string, style: TextStyle): number;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── layout ─────────────────────────────\n\nexport interface Rect { readonly x: number; readonly y: number; readonly width: number; readonly height: number }\n\nexport type LabelRemedy = 'thinned' | 'wrapped' | 'rotated' | 'truncated';\n\nexport interface PlacedText {\n  /** What is drawn (possibly truncated with an ellipsis in the middle). */\n  readonly lines: readonly string[];\n  /** The complete text, for tooltip and table. */\n  readonly full: string;\n  /** Anchor point in CSS px relative to the plot container. */\n  readonly x: number;\n  readonly y: number;\n  readonly anchor: 'start' | 'middle' | 'end';\n  readonly baseline: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom';\n  /** Degrees, clockwise; 0, -45 or -90 in 1.0. */\n  readonly rotate: number;\n  readonly role: 'tick-x' | 'tick-y' | 'title-x' | 'title-y' | 'value' | 'reference' | 'message';\n  /** Bounding box after rotation, for the overlap test. */\n  readonly box: Rect;\n}\n\n/** The mapping from data to pixels that scene building and lookup must share. */\nexport type Projection =\n  | { readonly kind: 'xy'; readonly x: XScale; readonly y: ContinuousScale }\n  /** Bars: `band` runs along the category axis, `value` along the other; `horizontal` swaps the screen axes. */\n  | { readonly kind: 'bands'; readonly horizontal: boolean; readonly band: XScale; readonly value: ContinuousScale }\n  | { readonly kind: 'ring'; readonly cx: number; readonly cy: number; readonly r0: number; readonly r1: number };\n\n/** What label fitting did on one axis, in the order it did it (spec L1). */\nexport interface AxisLabelFit {\n  readonly remedies: readonly LabelRemedy[];\n  /** Labels that could not be shown after every remedy. More than zero means the reader gets the L1 note. */\n  readonly hidden: number;\n}\n\ninterface LayoutSize {\n  /** Size of the plot container (the element that holds marks and tick text), CSS px. */\n  readonly width: number;\n  readonly height: number;\n}\n\n/**\n * Layout of the plot container only. The title, summary, legend, controls, notes and table are ordinary\n * HTML around it and are laid out by the browser; the plot container's size is whatever is left.\n */\nexport type Layout =\n  | (LayoutSize & {\n      readonly kind: 'plot';\n      /** Where marks are drawn. */\n      readonly plot: Rect;\n      readonly projection: Projection;\n      readonly xTicks: readonly Tick[];\n      readonly yTicks: readonly Tick[];\n      readonly texts: readonly PlacedText[];\n      readonly fit: { readonly x: AxisLabelFit; readonly y: AxisLabelFit };\n    })\n  /** Compact variant (spec 3.7): marks fill the container, no text. */\n  | (LayoutSize & { readonly kind: 'compact'; readonly plot: Rect; readonly projection: Projection })\n  /** The plot would be below 120 × 80 CSS px (spec L8): only the summary and the table control are shown. */\n  | (LayoutSize & { readonly kind: 'too-small' })\n  /** Loading, empty or error state (spec L7): a message instead of marks. */\n  | (LayoutSize & { readonly kind: 'message'; readonly text: PlacedText })\n  /** The container has no size yet (hidden or detached). Nothing is drawn until it gets one. */\n  | (LayoutSize & { readonly kind: 'unmeasured' });\n\n// ───────────────────────────── scene ─────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * A color that is resolved late, so an SVG renderer can use CSS variables and a canvas renderer can look\n * the value up in the resolved theme.\n */\nexport type Paint =\n  | { readonly kind: 'series'; readonly index: number }   // palette slot or the author's color for that series\n  | { readonly kind: 'token'; readonly name: 'axis' | 'grid' | 'reference' | 'muted' | 'focus' | 'background' | 'text' }\n  | { readonly kind: 'none' };\n\nexport type MarkerShape = 'circle' | 'square' | 'triangle' | 'diamond' | 'cross' | 'plus' | 'triangle-down' | 'star';\n\n/** Dash pattern in normal mode and under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors. null = solid. */\nexport interface DashStyle {\n  readonly normal: readonly number[] | null;\n  readonly contrast: readonly number[] | null;\n}\n\n/** A polyline. `xy` is x0,y0,x1,y1,… in CSS px; a NaN pair lifts the pen. */\nexport interface PathMark {\n  readonly type: 'path';\n  readonly series: number;\n  readonly xy: Float32Array;\n  readonly stroke: Paint;\n  readonly strokeWidth: number;\n  readonly dash: DashStyle;\n}\n\n/**\n * Filled regions between an upper and a lower boundary (area, stacked area). Each boundary is its own packed\n * polyline, so each can keep its own extremes when simplified (spec F5), and adjacent stack bands can share\n * the very same boundary array. `runs` holds quadruples (upperStart, upperCount, lowerStart, lowerCount) in\n * vertices; one quadruple is one polygon: its upper vertices forward, then its lower vertices in reverse.\n */\nexport interface AreaMark {\n  readonly type: 'area';\n  readonly series: number;\n  readonly upper: Float32Array;\n  readonly lower: Float32Array;\n  readonly runs: Uint32Array;\n  readonly fill: Paint;\n  readonly opacity: number;\n}\n\n/** Axis-aligned rectangles: x,y,width,height quadruples in CSS px. */\nexport interface RectMark {\n  readonly type: 'rects';\n  readonly series: number;\n  readonly xywh: Float32Array;\n  readonly fill: Paint;\n  /** 'missing' draws the \"no data\" mark of spec F2 instead of a filled bar. */\n  readonly variant: 'bar' | 'missing';\n}\n\n/** Markers: x,y pairs in CSS px. */\nexport interface PointMark {\n  readonly type: 'points';\n  readonly series: number;\n  readonly xy: Float32Array;\n  readonly shape: MarkerShape;\n  readonly size: number;\n  readonly fill: Paint;\n}\n\n/** Annular sectors (donut). Angles in radians, clockwise from 12 o'clock. */\nexport interface ArcMark {\n  readonly type: 'arcs';\n  readonly cx: number;\n  readonly cy: number;\n  readonly r0: number;\n  readonly r1: number;\n  readonly angles: Float32Array; // start0,end0,start1,end1,…\n  readonly fills: readonly Paint[];\n  /** Gap between adjacent slices in CSS px (spec A5). */\n  readonly gap: number;\n}\n\nexport interface RuleMark {\n  readonly type: 'rule';\n  readonly x1: number; readonly y1: number; readonly x2: number; readonly y2: number;\n  readonly stroke: Paint;\n  readonly strokeWidth: number;\n  readonly dash: readonly number[] | null;\n  readonly role: 'grid' | 'axis' | 'tick' | 'reference' | 'cursor' | 'break';\n}\n\n/** A filled rectangle that is not data: a reference band. */\nexport interface BandMark {\n  readonly type: 'band';\n  readonly box: Rect;\n  readonly fill: Paint;\n  readonly opacity: number;\n}\n\nexport type Mark = PathMark | AreaMark | RectMark | PointMark | ArcMark | RuleMark | BandMark;\n\n/** Everything the mark renderer draws, back to front. Plain data: no functions, no DOM. */\nexport interface Scene {\n  readonly width: number;\n  readonly height: number;\n  /** Marks are clipped to this rectangle unless their role is 'axis' or 'tick'. */\n  readonly clip: Rect;\n  readonly marks: readonly Mark[];\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── theme ─────────────────────────────\n\nexport type PaintToken = 'axis' | 'grid' | 'reference' | 'muted' | 'focus' | 'background' | 'text';\n\n/** CSS color expressions per paint. May be `var(--axylo-series-1)` or an author's `var(--brand-1)`: unresolved. */\nexport interface PaintBindings {\n  readonly tokens: Readonly<Record<PaintToken, string>>;\n  /** One expression per series index, already including the author's `colors` by series key. */\n  readonly series: readonly string[];\n}\n\n/** The same paints as concrete computed colors, plus what was detected about the page (spec L4, DX6). */\nexport interface ResolvedTheme {\n  readonly scheme: 'light' | 'dark';\n  readonly forcedColors: boolean;\n  readonly moreContrast: boolean;\n  readonly paints: PaintBindings;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── rendering ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** Transient decoration drawn above the scene: cursor rule and highlighted points (clipped to the plot), focus ring (not clipped). */\nexport interface Overlay {\n  readonly clip: Rect;\n  readonly clipped: readonly Mark[];\n  readonly unclipped: readonly Mark[];\n}\n\n/**\n * Draws a scene's marks. Two implementations exist for the M1 comparison (canvas, svg); the rest of the\n * library does not know which one is in use.\n *\n * A canvas renderer paints with `theme.paints` (concrete colors) and must be told to draw again when the\n * theme changes. An SVG renderer paints with `bindings` (CSS expressions), so the browser restyles it on a\n * theme change without a call; it switches dash patterns with CSS media queries.\n */\nexport interface MarkRenderer {\n  readonly kind: 'canvas' | 'svg';\n  /** The element to insert into the plot container. It must be aria-hidden. */\n  readonly element: HTMLElement | SVGSVGElement;\n  /** Draws the scene, replacing the previous one. Called at most once per animation frame. */\n  draw(scene: Scene, bindings: PaintBindings, theme: ResolvedTheme, devicePixelRatio: number): void;\n  /** Draws or clears (null) the overlay without redrawing the scene. */\n  drawOverlay(overlay: Overlay | null, bindings: PaintBindings, theme: ResolvedTheme, devicePixelRatio: number): void;\n  destroy(): void;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── lookup ─────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * A datum by identity, not by position, so it still means the same thing after a streaming window slides.\n * Valid within one frame epoch.\n */\nexport type DatumRef =\n  | { readonly kind: 'aligned'; readonly seriesKey: string; /** x position (category: its index at the time; categories do not slide). */ readonly x: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'point'; readonly seriesKey: string; /** source row index */ readonly row: number }\n  | { readonly kind: 'slice'; readonly sliceKey: string };\n\n/** What the pointer is over, by the picking rule of the chart type (spec 3.6). */\nexport type PickTarget =\n  | { readonly kind: 'x'; readonly x: number }                      // line, area, bar: every series at this x\n  | { readonly kind: 'point'; readonly refs: readonly DatumRef[]; readonly count: number }  // scatter: nearest point and those coinciding with it (at most 8; `count` is the full number)\n  | { readonly kind: 'slice'; readonly sliceKey: string };\n\n/** One row of a tooltip or one announced point, as text. All text comes from the frame through the formatters. */\nexport interface DatumText {\n  readonly ref: DatumRef;\n  readonly seriesIndex: number;\n  readonly seriesLabel: string;\n  /** x as the reader sees it. */\n  readonly x: string;\n  /** The value with its unit, or \"No data\" / \"No observation\". */\n  readonly value: string;\n  readonly state: SlotState;\n  /** Share of the whole for a donut slice, formatted; null elsewhere. */\n  readonly percent: string | null;\n  /** Source row index, or -1. */\n  readonly row: number;\n}\n\nexport interface TooltipModel {\n  /** Heading: the x value (with its time zone for instants), the category, or the slice label. */\n  readonly title: string;\n  /** At most eight rows (spec I1). */\n  readonly rows: readonly DatumText[];\n  /** Rows before truncation. */\n  readonly count: number;\n  /** Stack total with \"incomplete\" where it applies, already formatted; null when the chart does not stack. */\n  readonly total: string | null;\n}\n\nexport type StepDirection = 'next' | 'previous' | 'first' | 'last' | 'series-next' | 'series-previous' | 'page-next' | 'page-previous';\n\n/**\n * Exact answers from the frame for one view and projection, independent of how the scene was simplified.\n * Built once per (view, layout); cheap to build: binary search over x for aligned frames, a grid index for scatter.\n */\nexport interface Lookup {\n  /** What is under a pixel of the plot container, or null. */\n  pick(px: number, py: number): PickTarget | null;\n  tooltip(target: PickTarget): TooltipModel;\n  /** The datum to focus when the keyboard takes over from a pointer target. */\n  primary(target: PickTarget): DatumRef | null;\n  datum(ref: DatumRef): DatumText | null;\n  /** Where the datum is drawn, for the cursor and focus ring; null if it is not visible. */\n  anchor(ref: DatumRef): Rect | null;\n  /** Number of data the keyboard can reach in the current view, and a datum's place among them (zero based, -1 if absent). */\n  readonly count: number;\n  ordinal(ref: DatumRef): number;\n  /** Keyboard order per chart type (spec 3.6). `from` null starts at the first datum. Skips hidden series. */\n  step(from: DatumRef | null, direction: StepDirection): DatumRef | null;\n  /** After the data moved on: the same datum if it is still there, else the next one that is, else the previous, else null. */\n  resolve(ref: DatumRef): DatumRef | null;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── reader model ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** The table of spec A4, built lazily from the frame: nothing is materialized until a page is asked for. */\nexport interface TableModel {\n  readonly caption: string;\n  /** First entry is the x column. */\n  readonly headers: readonly string[];\n  readonly rowCount: number;\n  readonly pageSize: number;\n  /** Cells of one row as text; first cell is the row header. null outside the range. */\n  row(ordinal: number): readonly string[] | null;\n  /** Zero-based page that contains the first row at or after an x value; null if there is none. */\n  pageForX(x: XValue): number | null;\n}\n\n/** Everything a reader gets as text (spec A1, C1). */\nexport interface ReaderModel {\n  /** Accessible name: the title, or a generated one. Empty when the chart is named by `labelledBy`. */\n  readonly name: string;\n  /** Generated summary plus the author's description, including time zone and omission statements. */\n  readonly summary: string;\n  /** The reader's note of spec F12 and L1, or null. */\n  readonly note: string | null;\n  /** null for the compact variant and when the table is turned off. */\n  readonly table: TableModel | null;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── environment ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** Everything outside the options and data that output depends on. Part of every cache key that needs it. */\nexport interface Environment {\n  readonly locale: string;\n  /** The viewer's zone, used when a time field says 'local'. */\n  readonly timeZone: string;\n  readonly devicePixelRatio: number;\n  readonly reducedMotion: boolean;\n  readonly textStyle: TextStyle;\n  /** Increases whenever web fonts finish loading; part of the layout cache key. */\n  readonly fontEpoch: number;\n  readonly development: boolean;\n}\n\nexport type { Diagnostic, XValue };\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat packages/axylo/src/public.ts && sed -n '89,208p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '166,215p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"/**\n * Public types of Axylo. The functions that use them are exported from index.ts.\n * Source of truth for the public API; docs/api/axylo.d.ts is the reviewed sketch this file started from.\n */\n\n// ---------- data ----------\n\n/** Rows as an API returns them, or typed columns for large data. Both describe the same logical table. */\nexport type Data<Row> = readonly Row[] | { readonly columns: { readonly [K in keyof Row]: ArrayLike<Row[K]> } };\n\ntype Key<Row> = Extract<keyof Row, string>;\ntype KeysWhere<Row, V> = { [K in Key<Row>]: [Row[K]] extends [V] ? K : never }[Key<Row>];\n\n/** A field that can sit on a value axis as it is. */\nexport type NumberKey<Row> = KeysWhere<Row, number | null | undefined>;\n/** A field of numeric strings (Postgres bigint and numeric, BigQuery): allowed only with `parse: 'number'`. */\nexport type StringKey<Row> = KeysWhere<Row, string | null | undefined>;\n/** A field that can sit on an x axis: numbers, instants, calendar dates or categories. */\nexport type XKey<Row> = KeysWhere<Row, number | string | Date | null | undefined>;\n/** A field that names a series. */\nexport type SeriesKey<Row> = KeysWhere<Row, string | number | boolean>;\n\nexport type XValue = number | string | Date;\n\n// ---------- fields ----------\n\ninterface FieldCommon {\n  /** Axis title and the name used in tooltip, table and announcements. */\n  label?: string;\n  /** Unit appended to values in tooltip, table and announcements: 'ms', 'USD', … */\n  unit?: string;\n}\n\nexport interface ValueFieldOptions extends FieldCommon {\n  /** Declares percentage data and its convention: 'percent' = 0–100, 'ratio' = 0–1. Never inferred from the label. */\n  scale?: 'percent' | 'ratio';\n  /** How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: locale grouping, up to 6 significant fraction digits. */\n  format?: Intl.NumberFormatOptions | ((value: number) => string);\n  /** How axis ticks are written. Default: compact when it saves space. */\n  tickFormat?: Intl.NumberFormatOptions | ((value: number) => string);\n  /** What a missing value means. 'gap' (default) draws nothing; 'zero' is for counts where absence means none. */\n  missing?: 'gap' | 'zero';\n  /** Author bounds. On bar and area the domain is widened to include zero and AX110 is raised if it did not. */\n  domain?: readonly [number, number];\n}\n\nexport type ValueField<Row> =\n  | NumberKey<Row>\n  | ({ field: NumberKey<Row>; parse?: never } & ValueFieldOptions)\n  | ({ field: StringKey<Row>; parse: 'number' } & ValueFieldOptions);\n\nexport interface XFieldOptions extends FieldCommon {\n  /**\n   * 'date': calendar dates ('2026-02-24'); read the same in every time zone.\n   * 'time': instants (epoch milliseconds, Date objects, ISO strings with an offset); shown in `timeZone`.\n   * 'number' and 'category' as named. Inferred when omitted, except that numbers are never inferred to be instants.\n   */\n  type?: 'number' | 'time' | 'date' | 'category';\n  /** Category axes only: the categories have a natural order (months, age bands), so labels may be thinned when they do not fit. */\n  ordered?: boolean;\n  /** For instants only. Default 'local' (the viewer's zone). */\n  timeZone?: 'local' | 'UTC' | (string & {});\n  format?: Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions | Intl.NumberFormatOptions | ((value: XValue) => string);\n  tickFormat?: Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions | Intl.NumberFormatOptions | ((value: XValue) => string);\n  domain?: readonly [XValue, XValue];\n}\n\nexport type XField<Row> = XKey<Row> | ({ field: XKey<Row> } & XFieldOptions);\n\n/** Long form: one row per (x, series). Wide form: one row per x, one column per series. Never both. */\nexport type Encoding<Row> =\n  | { x: XField<Row>; y: ValueField<Row>; series?: SeriesKey<Row> }\n  | { x: XField<Row>; y: readonly ValueField<Row>[]; series?: never };\n\n// ---------- options ----------\n\nexport interface Title { text: string; visible?: boolean; level?: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 }\n\nexport interface CommonOptions<Row> {\n  data: Data<Row>;\n  /** Visible heading and accessible name. */\n  title?: string | Title;\n  /** Id of an existing heading that names the chart, for dashboard cards that already show a title. */\n  labelledBy?: string;\n  subtitle?: string;\n  /** Added to the generated summary. Mandatory disclosures (time zone, omitted values) are kept. */\n  description?: string;\n  state?: 'ready' | 'loading' | { error: string } | { empty: string };\n  legend?: 'auto' | false | 'top' | 'right' | 'bottom';\n  tooltip?: boolean;\n  /** \"View as table\" control. Default true. */\n  table?: boolean;\n  /** Fixed color per series name, so \"Direct\" is the same color on every chart: { Direct: 'var(--brand-1)' }. */\n  colors?: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;\n  /** Overrides for built-in text (\"View as table\", \"No data\", …). */\n  messages?: Partial<Messages>;\n  /** Default: the document language. */\n  locale?: string;\n  /** Throw on any warning or error diagnostic, synchronously from the call that caused it. */\n  strict?: boolean;\n  onDiagnostic?: (diagnostic: Diagnostic) => void;\n}\n\nexport interface Reference {\n  x?: XValue | readonly [XValue, XValue];\n  y?: number | readonly [number, number];\n  label: string;\n}\n\nexport interface CartesianOptions {\n  /** Default true on time, date and number x axes. 'touch' also captures pinch and two-finger drag on touch screens. */\n  zoom?: boolean | 'touch';\n  reference?: readonly Reference[];\n}\n\n/** Line, area and bar only. Marks only, as a static image with a text alternative: no axes, legend, toolbar, table, zoom or tooltip. */\nexport interface CompactOption { compact?: boolean }\n\nexport type LineOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & CompactOption & Encoding<Row>;\nexport type AreaOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & CompactOption & Encoding<Row> & { stacked?: boolean };\nexport type BarOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & CompactOption & Encoding<Row> & { layout?: 'grouped' | 'stacked'; horizontal?: boolean };\n/** Scatter relates two measures: its x is a number, an instant or a date, never a category. */\nexport type ScatterXField<Row> = XKey<Row> | ({ field: XKey<Row>; type?: 'number' | 'time' | 'date'; ordered?: never } & Omit<XFieldOptions, 'type' | 'ordered'>);\nexport type ScatterOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & { x: ScatterXField<Row>; y: ValueField<Row>; series?: SeriesKey<Row> };\nexport type DonutOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & { label: SeriesKey<Row>; value: ValueField<Row> };\n\n// ---------- handle ----------\n\nexport interface Point<Row> { series: string; x: XValue; y: number | null; row: Row | null; index: number }\n\nexport interface ChartEvents<Row> {\n  hover: Point<Row> | null;\n  select: Point<Row>;\n  zoom: readonly [XValue, XValue] | null;\n}\n\nexport interface Chart<Row, Options> {\n  /**\n   * Changes options. Keys present replace the old value; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay.\n   * Validation and diagnostics are synchronous (a `strict` chart throws here and keeps its last valid state); drawing happens on the next frame.\n   */\n  update(patch: Partial<Options>): void;\n  /** Adds rows at the end. `keep` = number of distinct x values retained; older ones are dropped. */\n  append(rows: readonly Row[], options?: { keep?: number }): void;\n  /** Sets the visible x range; `null` shows everything. */\n  zoom(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] | null): void;\n  on<E extends keyof ChartEvents<Row>>(event: E, listener: (payload: ChartEvents<Row>[E]) => void): () => void;\n  /** Diagnostics for the current options and data. Up to date as soon as create, update or append returns. */\n  readonly diagnostics: readonly Diagnostic[];\n  destroy(): void;\n}\n\nexport type Target = string | HTMLElement;\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n// ---------- diagnostics ----------\n\nexport interface Diagnostic {\n  /** Stable and documented, one page per code. */\n  code: `AX${number}`;\n  level: 'error' | 'warning' | 'hint';\n  /** What is wrong, where, and how to fix it. */\n  message: string;\n  /** The chart it came from: its title, else its element id. */\n  chart: string;\n  /** Where applicable. A diagnostic about the chart as a whole (no title, low contrast) has neither. */\n  field?: string;\n  /** First offending row indexes in the caller's own data, capped at 10. */\n  rows?: readonly number[];\n  count?: number;\n}\n\n/** Runs normalization and validation only. Works without a DOM, so a server or an agent can check options before rendering. */\n\n/** Built-in text. Templates use {name} placeholders. Every entry can be replaced through `messages` (spec A10). */\nexport interface Messages {\n  viewAsTable: string; hideTable: string;\n  noData: string; noObservation: string; incompleteStack: string;\n  moreRows: string; otherSlices: string;\n  valuesNotShown: string; valuesChanged: string; labelsHidden: string;\n  loading: string; empty: string; error: string; internalError: string;\n  chartName: string; chartSummary: string; seriesSummary: string; referenceSummary: string; timeZoneNote: string;\n  plotLabel: string; controlsLabel: string; pointOf: string;\n  zoomIn: string; zoomOut: string; resetZoom: string; pause: string; resume: string;\n  previousPage: string; nextPage: string; pageOf: string; jumpToX: string;\n  showSeries: string; hideSeries: string;\n}\n\n### 3.1 Faithful representation\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A gap is drawn whenever at least one whole column contains no valid sample. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, reference lines and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices, the seven largest are drawn and the rest are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; the table lists every category. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker, and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"2 values not shown. See table.\") inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n\n### 3.2 Legibility\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to two lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\n| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\n| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, the one-line summary and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n\n### 3.3 Interaction\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". When it cannot fit, the same content appears in a fixed readout under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\n| I5 | First render is not animated. An update may transition for at most 200 ms. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions, on first render or on update. | After `document.fonts.ready`: first frame equals the frame at 2.5 s within a 0.1% pixel tolerance, in both settings; an update under reduced motion reaches its final frame in one step; a 24-chart filter change settles within 200 ms | R14 |\n| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n\n### 3.4 Accessibility\n\nTarget: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo renders, as component-level evidence. Conformance of a page is a property of the page and remains the author's.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n\n### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n\nLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n\n| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time or date axis | Gaps |\n| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n\nExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n\nThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n\n### 3.6 Type matrix\n\n| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n| Zoom | Yes | Yes | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n\n### 3.7 Compact variant\n\n`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### 2.1 Pipeline\n\n```\noptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n                                         ├─► summary and table\n                                         └─► focus model\n```\n\nThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n\n| Stage | Output | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n\n### 2.2 Invalidation\n\nStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n\n| Stage | Reruns when |\n|---|---|\n| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n| scene | Layout or view model. |\n| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n\nText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n\n### 2.3 Rendering\n\n**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n\n**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n\nM1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n\n**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\n\n**No animation system.** First render is not animated (I5). Updates cut, or tween for at most 200 ms where the cost is negligible, and not at all under reduced motion.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
